        result = _asn1_decode_simple_ber(etype, p, der_len, &out, &out_len, &tmp_len, flags);
        if (result != ASN1_SUCCESS)
        {
            warn();
            goto cleanup;
        }
        p += tmp_len;
        DECR_LEN(der_len, tmp_len);
        if (ber_len) *ber_len += tmp_len;
        DECR_LEN(der_len, 2); /* we need the EOC */
        if (out_len > 0)
        {
            result = append(&total, &total_size, out, out_len);
            if (result != ASN1_SUCCESS)
            {
                warn();
                goto cleanup;
            }
        }
        free(out);
        out = NULL;
        if (p[0] == 0 && p[1] == 0) /* EOC */
        {
            if (ber_len) *ber_len += 2;
            break;
        }
        /* no EOC */
        der_len += 2;
        if (der_len == 2)
        {
            warn();
            result = ASN1_DER_ERROR;
            goto cleanup;
        }
    } while(1);
}
```

Wait, I see a potential issue! After `DECR_LEN(der_len, 2)` (which decrements `der_len` by 2 for the EOC), the code checks if `p[0] == 0 && p[1] == 0`. If it's not the EOC, it does `der_len += 2` to add back the 2 bytes. But `p` was already advanced past the data, so `p[0]` and `p[1]` are the next bytes in the DER stream.

But there's a subtle issue: after `DECR_LEN(der_len, 2)`, `der_len` is decremented by 2. If `p[0]` and `p[1]` are not EOC, `der_len += 2` adds them back. But what if `der_len` was already 2 before the `DECR_LEN`? Then after `DECR_LEN`, `der_len = 0`, and the check `if (der_len == 2)` would be false. Then `der_len += 2` makes it 2 again, and the loop continues. But `p` points to the bytes after the data, and `der_len` is 2, so the next iteration would try to decode from `p` with `der_len = 2`. This could lead to reading uninitialized data if `p` points to uninitialized memory.

But wait, `p` points into the DER buffer, which is initialized. So this shouldn't be an issue.

